\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li360\ri927\sl244\slmult1\sb237\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 The LBAs for the BB Chevy are 77.5 degrees between intake lifter bores and 90 degrees between exhaust lifter bores.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 That means the right bank\'92s intakes occur 77.5 deg before the #1 intake.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The #1 exhaust (and other exhausts on the left bank) occur (90-77.5) /\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 2\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 = 6.25 deg degrees after the #1 Intake (-6.25 deg).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The right\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 bank\'92s exhausts occur 77.5 deg +6.25 deg before #1 intake.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Using this info you will fill in the Lifter Bore Angle Details screen as shown in Figure A4.16 for the Big Block Chevy. (As stated earlier, Performance Trends has loaded in several of these details for several engines, including the BB Chevy, and you can just pick a Cam Design and avoid these inputs.)\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par
